{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":1933429,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,31]],"date-time":"2026-10-31T06:34:05Z","timestamp":1793428445154,"version":"4.5.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]],"date-time":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1767225600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.uspto.gov\/ip-policy\/copyright-policy\/copyright-basics"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present TransWave, a multiscale causal language-model family built around wavelet filtering\n&lt;br&gt;\nrather than token-to-token attention. The design has three properties that are usually treated\n&lt;br&gt;\nseparately: direct modeling of raw text without a learned subword vocabulary, causal memory\n&lt;br&gt;\nwith analytically nonzero influence at arbitrarily large finite lags in the stateful rational realization,\n&lt;br&gt;\nand a computation graph dominated by block-local transforms, tensor contractions, structured\n&lt;br&gt;\nconvolution, and associative scan. We evaluate two complementary realizations. TransWave-S is\n&lt;br&gt;\na 126,746-parameter stateful symbolic rational Meyer-approximation model. On a fixed Project\n&lt;br&gt;\nGutenberg validation set it outperforms parameter-matched Llama-, Qwen3-, Gemma3-, and\n&lt;br&gt;\nDeepSeek-V3-family models on every common normalized likelihood metric: byte NLL 1.6442,\n&lt;br&gt;\nbyte PPL 5.1767, BPB 2.3720, and NLL per lexical unit 12.1927. The strongest Transformer-\n&lt;br&gt;\nfamily baseline, Qwen3, obtains 2.3097, 10.0714, 3.3322, and 17.1281, respectively. TransWave-S\n&lt;br&gt;\ntherefore reduces the NLL\/BPB quantities by 28.81% and byte perplexity by 48.60% relative to the\n&lt;br&gt;\nstrongest baseline while using 559,991 scored lexical training positions versus 8,064,000 for every\n&lt;br&gt;\nTransformer baseline. TransWave-B is a separate 96.13M-parameter model that reads UTF-8 files\n&lt;br&gt;\ndirectly as bytes, uses no BPE\/SentencePiece\/WordPiece stage, forms learned block-local lifting\n&lt;br&gt;\nscales, and mixes them with a causal Meyer-structured FIR plus a local causal path. A completed\n&lt;br&gt;\n4,499,996,672-byte run achieves overall held-out NLL 2.1275, PPL 8.3942, and BPB 3.0694 across\n&lt;br&gt;\narXiv, FineWeb-Edu, PG19, and WikiText-103; corpus-specific PPL is below 9 on all four. We\n&lt;br&gt;\nprove exact causal factorization of TransWave-B and stable nonvanishing temporal support\n&lt;br&gt;\nof the stateful rational bank, and establish chunk-equivalence, reconstruction, and complexity\n&lt;br&gt;\nproperties. All reported training was performed on GPUs because of the available host\/runtime\n&lt;br&gt;\nresources. The architecture itself is not GPU-specific: both tokenization and learning expose regular\n&lt;br&gt;\nTPU-compatible parallelism, and the JAX realization uses sharded arrays and associative scan.\n&lt;br&gt;\nCode and machine-readable results are public.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7668678","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,31]],"date-time":"2026-10-31T06:13:11Z","timestamp":1793427191000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["TransWave: A Multiscale, Virtually Infinite-context, Attention-free Raw-Text Language Model via Highly Parallelized Causal Meyer Wavelet Filtering"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1065-3000","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Angshul","family":"Majumdar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,31]],"date-time":"2026-10-31T06:13:11Z","timestamp":1793427191000},"score":23.175892,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=7668678"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7668678","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,10]],"date-time":"2026-10-10T06:56:52Z","timestamp":1791615412079,"version":"4.3.4"},"reference-count":14,"publisher":"Informa UK Limited","issue":"1","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Communications in Algebra"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,12,26]]},"DOI":"10.1080\/00927870601041458","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2011,7,6]],"date-time":"2011-07-06T12:38:59Z","timestamp":1309955939000},"page":"171-180","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":18,"title":["Infinite Planar Zero-Divisor Graphs"],"prefix":"10.1080","volume":"35","author":[{"given":"Neal O.","family":"Smith","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"301","reference":[{"key":"CIT0001","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/S0021-8693(03)00370-3"},{"key":"CIT0002","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1006\/jabr.1993.1171"},{"key":"CIT0003","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1006\/jabr.1998.7840"},{"key":"CIT0004","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"Anderson , D. F. , Frazier , A. , Lauve , A. , Livingston , P. S. ( 2001 ).The Zero-Divisor Graph of a Commutative Ring, II. Lecture Notes in Pure and Applied Mathematics . Vol. 220 . New York : Dekker , pp. 61 \u2013 72 .","DOI":"10.1201\/9780429187902-5"},{"key":"CIT0005","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/S0022-4049(02)00250-5"},{"key":"CIT0006","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/0021-8693(88)90202-5"},{"key":"CIT0007","first-page":"93","volume":"1","author":"DeMeyer F.","year":"2002","journal-title":"International J. Comm. Rings"},{"key":"CIT0008","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/s002330010128"},{"key":"CIT0009","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/BF01362435"},{"key":"CIT0010","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/BF01359907"},{"key":"CIT0011","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1081\/AGB-120013178"},{"key":"CIT0012","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1081\/AGB-120004502"},{"key":"CIT0013","first-page":"203","volume":"1","author":"Redmond S. P.","year":"2002","journal-title":"International J. Comm. Rings"},{"key":"CIT0014","first-page":"177","volume":"2","author":"Smith N. O.","year":"2003","journal-title":"International J. Comm. Rings"}],"container-title":["Communications in Algebra"],"language":"en","link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/www.tandfonline.com\/doi\/pdf\/10.1080\/00927870601041458","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,21]],"date-time":"2020-06-21T05:04:37Z","timestamp":1592715877000},"score":22.656963,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"http:\/\/www.tandfonline.com\/doi\/abs\/10.1080\/00927870601041458"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,12,26]]},"references-count":14,"journal-issue":{"issue":"1","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,12,26]]}},"alternative-id":["10.1080\/00927870601041458"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/00927870601041458","ISSN":["0092-7872","1532-4125"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0092-7872","type":"print"},{"value":"1532-4125","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2006,12,26]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T13:12:51Z","timestamp":1791378771542,"version":"4.3.2"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"group-title":"Article","reference-count":0,"publisher":"The Conversation Media Group Ltd","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"DOI":"10.64628\/ab.jqh5nn7sq","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]],"date-time":"2025-10-29T14:56:28Z","timestamp":1761749788000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["\u2018Hallucinated\u2019 cases are affecting lawyers\u2019 careers \u2013 they need to be trained to use AI"],"prefix":"10.64628","author":[{"given":"Craig","family":"Smith","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Salford"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"54080","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]],"date-time":"2025-10-29T14:56:28Z","timestamp":1761749788000},"score":22.463745,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/theconversation.com\/hallucinated-cases-are-affecting-lawyers-careers-they-need-to-be-trained-to-use-ai-265898"}},"editor":[{"given":"Grace","family":"Allen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"editor"}]}],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64628\/ab.jqh5nn7sq","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"subtype":"other"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,11]],"date-time":"2026-03-11T07:39:11Z","timestamp":1773214751746,"version":"3.50.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Modeling long sequences efficiently and accurately remains a fundamental challenge in deep learning. While attention-based architectures excel at capturing local and irregular dependencies, their quadratic complexity limits scalability to long contexts and large streaming workloads. Conversely, linear-time sequence models based on selective state space dynamics scale efficiently and exhibit excellent long-range memory, yet often lack the representational precision and adaptive compositional reasoning needed for short-range interactions. This paper introduces HARMONY, a hybrid architecture that unifies local exact attention, selective state space modeling, and lightweight mixture-based specialization within a single calibrated residual framework. In contrast to prior hybrid approaches that heuristically stack heterogeneous modules, HARMONY employs adaptive residual gating to modulate gradient flow, control computational allocation, and dynamically route signals across pathways based on input statistics. This design yields a fine-grained division of labor in which attention handles short-range, content-sensitive dependencies, SSMs provide structured long-range memory, and micro-experts capture conditional specialization without the distributed overhead of large-scale MoE systems. We present a formal problem formulation, architectural construction, computational complexity analysis, and training methodology, together with an extensible evaluation blueprint for language, streaming, and multimodal benchmarks. The proposed architecture exhibits near-linear scaling with respect to sequence length while preserving strong local modeling fidelity and specialization capacity. As a consequence, HARMONY offers a promising direction for efficient long-context modeling in domains requiring both expressiveness and scalability.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6147230","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]],"date-time":"2026-03-10T09:47:23Z","timestamp":1773136043000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["HARMONY: Hybrid Attention-Selective State Space Mixture for Long-Context Deep Learning"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"John","family":"Smith","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Emma","family":"Davis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]],"date-time":"2026-03-10T09:47:23Z","timestamp":1773136043000},"score":21.669815,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=6147230"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6147230","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,4]],"date-time":"2026-08-04T10:54:04Z","timestamp":1785840844222,"version":"3.56.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]],"date-time":"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1767225600000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.uspto.gov\/ip-policy\/copyright-policy\/copyright-basics"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;\n Predicting localized crime probabilities in massive urban centers faces three intercon-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n nected challenges: incident frequencies are severely sparse and dominated by zeros, standard\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n grid architectures overlook robust spatial dependencies between adjacent areas, and incident\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n logs differ significantly in credibility. To address this, we propose ZIGAT-MAE, a graph at-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n tention architecture conditioned on contextual factors that estimates near-term crime proba-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n bilities across H3 hexagonal spatial networks. Instead of a dedicated temporal module, each\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n vertex synthesizes three distinct inputs (recent dynamic trends, fixed structural properties,\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n and external environmental variables) which are integrated via a dual-layer, edge-weighted\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n graph attention framework and subsequently adjusted by the environmental context using\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n a feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM) mechanism. The network generates parameters for\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n a Zero-Inflated Poisson (ZIP) distribution via dual specialized output heads. These heads\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n accommodate the distinct statistical natures of a localized, highly reliable data feed and a\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n broader, high-frequency stream, and are optimized together via a sparsity-conscious weighted\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n likelihood and dynamically balanced loss functions. To capitalize on urban spatial layouts,\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n the encoder undergoes initial multi-city pretraining utilizing a self-supervised masked au-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n toencoder (MAE) strategy to rebuild hidden static vertex features, coupled with a \u201cghost-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n imputation\u201d regularization technique that randomly diminishes untrustworthy historical data\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n during learning. When rigorously assessed across nine diverse Mexican metropolitan regions\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n using a strict chronological split preventing data leakage, ZIGAT-MAE achieves an aggregate\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n ROC-AUC of 0.942. It yields an average precision 194\u00d7 greater than the baseline prevalence\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n of 3.9\u00d710\u22124, while maintaining excellent calibration (rate-ECE and Brier scores under 10\u22123).\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n Practically, identifying merely the highest-risk 5% of hexagonal vertices during a given time\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n block successfully anticipates roughly 72% of actual incidents, demonstrating a statistically\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n robust improvement over a baseline of fixed historical hotspots. A unified model successfully\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n generalizes across all nine distinct urban landscapes. Furthermore, ablation studies indicate\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n that the self-supervised MAE pretraining and the ZIP-based supervised fine-tuning are syn-\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n ergistic, with the latter elevating the ROC-AUC from 0.600 to 0.942. Finally, our evaluation\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n highlights the severe geographic clustering of metropolitan crime (characterized by a Gini\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n coefficient of 0.96, where the uppermost 5% of locations experience approximately 89% of\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n all documented events), validating both the spatial patterns the architecture leverages and\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n the practical utility of these predictions for strategic resource deployment\n&lt;\/div&gt;<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7155845","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,4]],"date-time":"2026-08-04T10:40:23Z","timestamp":1785840023000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["ZIGAT-MAE: A Context-Conditioned Zero-Inflated Graph Attention Network with Masked Autoencoders for Spatio-Temporal Urban Crime-Risk Forecasting"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1428-6625","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Diego","family":"Salda\u00f1a-Ulloa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,4]],"date-time":"2026-08-04T10:40:23Z","timestamp":1785840023000},"score":21.641203,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=7155845"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7155845","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"}],"items-per-page":5,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}